window.docContents[41].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Fourier-analysis-on-the-Schwartz-dual":{"contents":"Compactly supported test functions are the right probes for local questions.\nFourier analysis instead uses the Schwartz space, whose functions and\nderivatives decay faster than every polynomial. Its continuous dual is the\nspace of tempered distributions. Mathlib supplies the space, its Fourier\ntransform and its support theory. Convolution with Schwartz functions,\ntranslation and pushforward are built on top of it.\n\nFor example, the Gaussian  is a Schwartz function: every\nderivative is a polynomial times the same Gaussian, so it still decays\nfaster than any inverse power. A polynomial need not decay at all, but it\ndefines a tempered distribution because integrating it against a Schwartz\nprobe is controlled by the probe's decay. Dirac masses and their derivatives\nare tempered too. “Tempered” therefore restricts growth at infinity rather\nthan requiring pointwise smoothness.\n\nThe Fourier convention matters when turning differentiation into algebra.\nFor integrable functions the kernel is , giving\n\n\n\nThe transposed operators extend these identities to tempered distributions.\nThe Sobolev chapter uses this frequency weight to measure derivatives of\nnoninteger order.\n\nA tempered distribution on a real normed space , with values in a complex\ntopological vector space , is a continuous complex\nlinear functional on the Schwartz space:Mathlib equips this dual with the topology of pointwise convergence, which\nis weaker than the strong dual topologies common in functional analysis.\nContinuity statements about tempered distributions refer to this topology.\n\nThe reason to pass to the Schwartz dual is that the Fourier transform preserves\n, so its transpose acts on . On a finite-dimensional\ninner-product space the transform is invertible on , and the\ntransposed maps inherit invertibility.\n\nOn a finite-dimensional real inner-product space, Fourier transform and inverse\nFourier transform form a continuous complex-linear equivalenceIts forward map is  and its inverse is\n.\n\nBoth composites being the identity is what the word equivalence records. The two\nlaws are stated separately because the two directions are used separately, for\ninstance when convolution is defined through  and then\ntransformed back.\n\nFor every tempered distribution ,\n\nMathlib first proves that Fourier transform and inverse Fourier transform form\nboth a FourierPair and a FourierInvPair on tempered distributions. The two\ngeneric pair identities are precisely the displayed inverse equations, and\nthey are the inverse laws used by the continuous linear equivalence.\n\nThe equivalence is characterized by its pairing with Schwartz functions.\n\nFourier transform on tempered distributions is the transpose of Fourier\ntransform on Schwartz functions:This equation fixes Mathlib's convention: the transpose uses the forward\nSchwartz transform itself, with no hidden inverse transform or reflection.\n\nThis is the defining evaluation rule for Mathlib's Fourier-transform instance\non the pointwise continuous dual of Schwartz space.\n\nThe derivative rule is the reason Fourier analysis is useful for\nconstant-coefficient equations: differentiation becomes multiplication by a\nlinear symbol.\n\nFor a complex normed space , , and a direction\n, distributional differentiation becomes multiplication by the\nFourier variable:where multiplication is understood as the canonical multiplier operator on\ntempered distributions.\n\nMove the distributional derivative onto a Schwartz test function, apply the\nSchwartz-space Fourier derivative rule, and transpose multiplication by the\nlinear coordinate . Mathlib states the\nresult as an equality of tempered distributions.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis\u0009Tempered Distributions","header":"3.4.2.1. Fourier analysis on the Schwartz dual","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Fourier-analysis-on-the-Schwartz-dual"},"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--How-the-subject-acquired-its-language":{"contents":"The higher heat modes suggest a way to handle more general initial data:\nexpand the temperature in a sine series and evolve each coefficient. For a\nfinite sum, differentiation and addition commute and the calculation is\nimmediate. For an infinite series, convergence, differentiation, and recovery\nof the initial function become part of the problem. This is an old source of\nnew analysis.\n\nD'Alembert's wave equation already raised the question: its solutions involve\narbitrary traveling profiles, while the eighteenth-century understanding of a\nfunction was tied to analytic expressions, and Euler, d'Alembert, and the\nBernoullis disagreed over which shapes could count as initial data. Fourier's\ntrigonometric series represented rough initial temperatures and evolved them\nby damping high frequencies long before convergence and termwise\ndifferentiation were understood. These two equations pushed analysis toward\nthe arbitrary function, rigorous integration, and Fourier analysis.\n\nPotential theory exposed a different difficulty. Dirichlet's principle\nrelated a harmonic function with prescribed boundary values to a minimizer of\nan energy. Riemann used the principle successfully, but Weierstrass objected\nto the existence argument: an energy can have an infimum without attaining\nit. Choosing functions whose energies approach the best possible value does\nnot yet produce a best function. Completeness, compactness, and lower\nsemicontinuity explain when that limiting step works. We will meet the same\nissue when constructing weak solutions from approximations.\n\nHilbert-space methods gave linear PDE an abstract setting, but limits still\nneed not preserve classical derivatives. Sobolev's weak derivatives made it\npossible to complete smooth functions in norms controlling both values and\nderivatives. Schwartz's distributions gave differentiation a setting in which\nit is always defined and continuous. Leray's Navier–Stokes construction\ncombined a weak formulation with energy control to obtain global solutions,\nwithout thereby obtaining global smoothness or uniqueness. This remains a\nproductive way to approach an equation: choose a solution concept stable\nunder the estimates, construct a solution there, and then investigate its\nregularity. Entropy and viscosity solutions make different choices because\nshocks and nondifferentiable value functions present different obstacles.\n\nComputer-assisted analysis creates another need for a usable mathematical\nobject. We may be able to inspect the idea of an estimate without reading\nevery one of its arithmetic steps. A certificate records data from which a\nchecker can prove the claimed identity or bound. The search for it can use\nheuristics; the accepted result has a precise mathematical meaning. PDEAtlas\nuses such certificates alongside weak derivatives and energy estimates, so\nthat a long calculation can enter an argument through an ordinary theorem.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Introduction","header":"1.2. How the subject acquired its language","id":"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--How-the-subject-acquired-its-language"}});